# LicenRoute: licences and quotas for an existing API

> Language of these instructions: **English (en)**. Reply to the developer in their language. Spanish version:
> `./node_modules/.bin/licenroute assistant <dir> --claude-code|--cursor --lang es` (same rules).

Use it so that an **Express 4 or 5** (Node.js) API charges for, licenses or limits by quota the access to some of its
routes, with LicenRoute; in the Dev Preview, against a local simulator (demo mode, no Docker). You (the assistant) guide the developer; **the decisions are theirs**.

## 1. First of all: is this a case for LicenRoute?

Use LicenRoute only if the developer wants **third parties to pay, hold a licence or have a quota** to use
routes of their API (e.g. "make them pay to query /api/reports", "100 free queries per customer and then pay",
"licences for agents that read my data").

**Do not recommend it** —and say so clearly— when:

- they only want registered users to get in (login, sessions, roles): use their framework's authentication;
- they want to curb abuse or traffic without charging (simple rate limiting, DDoS, WAF, bots): their server, a proxy or their CDN;
- their backend is not Express 4/5 (Rails, Fastify, NestJS, Django, Laravel…): explain it and do not force anything. **Rails is
  out of scope for this distribution**: do not propose `init`, `apply` or changes to a Rails app, even if `plan`
  analyses it;
- they already have another charging solution that works and have not asked to change it.

When in doubt, ask what problem they want to solve before running anything. Details in
`decision-guide.md` (@.cursor/licenroute/decision-guide.md).

## 2. Requirements and tools

- LicenRoute Dev Preview, installed by the developer as a development dependency of the project, from the tarballs that
  `npm run build` leaves in `dist-packages/` of the LicenRoute repository: `@licenroute/cli` (commands `licenroute` and
  `licenroute-simulator`, the local simulator for demo mode) and the Express SDK `@licenroute/connect-express`. **Demo mode =
  local simulation: it protects and charges nothing real.**
- **Run LicenRoute only from that local installation**, from the project root: `./node_modules/.bin/licenroute …`. Before the
  first command, check it: `npm ls @licenroute/cli` must list the package. If it is missing, **STOP** and tell the
  developer. Do not install it yourself and **never** use `npx licenroute`, `npx licenroute-simulator` or `npm install licenroute`:
  on npm the unscoped name `licenroute` is not this package (anyone may publish under it), and without an interactive terminal `npx` downloads and
  runs a missing package without asking.
- Check the version: `./node_modules/.bin/licenroute --version` must list `detect/v1 plan/v1`. If not, stop and say so.
  Installing this Skill or the Cursor rules: `./node_modules/.bin/licenroute assistant <dir> --claude-code|--cursor`
